Const Generics: This Rust Feature Cut Our Code by 85% An Afternoon That Raised My Blood Pressure An old colleague asked me to grab lunch last week. Over dinner, he mentioned a cryptography library he recently took over.
&ldquo;You won&rsquo;t believe it,&rdquo; he put down his chopsticks, exhaustion in his voice. &ldquo;The code is an absolute disaster.&rdquo;
I asked what happened.
He said their team wrote a face recognition library three years ago. To support matrix operations of different sizes, they used macros to generate 16 almost identical codebases. Sixteen! Each one just changed a number — 16, 32, 64, 128&hellip; like Russian nesting dolls.
&ldquo;Do you know how many lines we ended up with?&rdquo; He held up a finger. &ldquo;8,347 lines. And that was after trimming.&rdquo;
I thought to myself, this isn&rsquo;t coding, it&rsquo;s code graveyard.
&ldquo;What happened later?&rdquo; I asked.
&ldquo;Later?&rdquo; He gave a bitter laugh. &ldquo;Every time I fixed a bug, I had to make the same change in 16 places. Forget one place, and the production server crashed the next day. I lost a third of my hair in six months — look at me now.&rdquo;
I looked at his noticeably thinning hair and sighed internally.
Until Rust 1.51 Arrived &ldquo;The turning point came in March 2021,&rdquo; he took a sip of soup, his eyes finally lighting up. &ldquo;Rust 1.51 stabilized const generics.&rdquo;
I was confused. &ldquo;Const what?&rdquo;
He wiped his mouth and started explaining. &ldquo;Ever heard of tailor-made clothing? Back in the day, after taking your measurements — height, shoulder width, waist — the tailor would cut a piece of fabric just for you. But Rust&rsquo;s generic system had a big bug before — it could only tell you &rsquo;this is a piece of clothing,&rsquo; but couldn&rsquo;t tell you &rsquo;this is a size XL.'&rdquo;
&ldquo;Is that it?&rdquo; I thought he was making a big deal out of nothing. &ldquo;Hear me out,&rdquo; he waved his hand. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s not just about changing sizes. You know what? Before this, if you wanted to write a function that handles fixed-size arrays, you couldn&rsquo;t write generic code.&rdquo;
He grabbed a napkin and sketched a diagram:
// Before: Want a generic function? No way fn process_16(data: [u8; 16]) { /* process 16 bytes */ } fn process_32(data: [u8; 32]) { /* process 32 bytes */ } fn process_64(data: [u8; 64]) { /* process 64 bytes */ } // ... write 16 functions like this &ldquo;See?&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Each function only has a different number, the logic is identical. But the compiler doesn&rsquo;t buy it. It says 16 and 32 are different types — you have to write them separately.&rdquo;
I got it. It&rsquo;s like going to a courier service and they say, &ldquo;Sorry, we only recognize box models, not box sizes.&rdquo; You say you want to ship a small box, and they say, &ldquo;For small boxes, we have Type A, Type B, Type C&hellip; Which one do you want?&rdquo;
&ldquo;So how does const generics solve this?&rdquo; I was getting interested.
He smiled: &ldquo;It lets the compiler know that 16, 32, and 64 are actually family.&rdquo;
// Now: One function handles all sizes fn process&lt;const N: usize&gt;(data: [u8; N]) { // N is the array size, known at compile time } That&rsquo;s it? One const N: usize turns 16 functions into 1.
&ldquo;That&rsquo;s literally it,&rdquo; he nodded vigorously. &ldquo;When I saw this syntax, I was stunned. Three years! We struggled for three years, just waiting for this syntax sugar.&rdquo;
How 85% of the Code Disappeared After lunch, we moved to a coffee shop. He insisted on showing me their refactored data. &ldquo;Do you know how many lines we have now?&rdquo; He handed me his phone. &ldquo;1,243 lines.&rdquo;
I calculated — from 8,347 to 1,243, that&rsquo;s about 85% less. &ldquo;But that&rsquo;s not the scariest part,&rdquo; he said mysteriously. &ldquo;Guess what the performance is like?&rdquo;
I shook my head.
&ldquo;Small array operations are 83% faster. Compile time dropped from 23 seconds to 9 seconds. Binary size went from 847KB to 507KB.&rdquo;
I almost spat out my coffee: &ldquo;Wait, 85% less code, but almost double the performance? You&rsquo;re kidding?&rdquo;
He shook his head seriously. &ldquo;No kidding. There are two reasons. First, const generics lets the compiler do optimizations it couldn&rsquo;t do before. Like SIMD auto-vectorization — once the compiler knows the array size, it can fully unroll loops and prefetch data.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And the second?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Second,&rdquo; he lowered his voice, &ldquo;the code generated by macros had a lot of redundant memory operations. With const generics, the compiler can do better inlining and constant folding. A lot of computation happens at compile time, not runtime.&rdquo;
He opened his laptop and showed me some code:
// Before: 16 implementations, each doing dynamic allocation fn multiply_16(a: &amp;[f32; 16], b: &amp;[f32; 16]) -&gt; Vec&lt;f32&gt; { let mut result = Vec::with_capacity(16); // heap allocation! // ... calculation result } // Now: Stack-based, zero allocation fn multiply&lt;const N: usize&gt;(a: &amp;[f32; N], b: &amp;[f32; N]) -&gt; [f32; N] { let mut result = [0.0; N]; // stack allocation, size known at compile time // ... same calculation result } &ldquo;See?&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Before, every matrix multiplication had to allocate new memory on the heap. Now it computes directly on the stack. Memory allocation is expensive — if you can save it, you should.&rdquo;
Const Generics in Everyday Life I asked him to explain const generics with an everyday scenario. He thought for a moment and said, &ldquo;Ever bought flat-pack furniture?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yeah, IKEA.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You know IKEA cabinets, right? Each model has a fixed number of parts. The instruction manual says: cabinet A needs 4 side panels, 2 top panels, 3 shelves&hellip; But if you wanted to make a universal assembly robot, what would you do?&rdquo;
I shook my head. &ldquo;Before, you&rsquo;d write a separate program for each cabinet model. Cabinet A uses program A, cabinet B uses program B, cabinet C uses program C&hellip; 16 models, 16 programs.&rdquo;
&ldquo;After const generics, you just write one program: cabinet{side_panels: 4, top_panels: 2, shelf_panels: 3}. Then tell the robot, this is model 16, that one is model 32, and the robot looks up the config to know what parts it needs.&rdquo;
I got it: &ldquo;So const generics parameterizes those &lsquo;models&rsquo;?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Exactly,&rdquo; he was happy I understood. &ldquo;Models are constants. The number and dimensions of models can be passed as type parameters. The compiler can immediately see how big the cabinet is and how many panels it needs, no runtime lookup required.&rdquo;
Those Head-Scratching Limitations &ldquo;Though,&rdquo; I poured some cold water, &ldquo;there must be limits, right?&rdquo;
He nodded. &ldquo;Of course. You can&rsquo;t put arbitrary operations in const parameters. For example, if you want to write impl&lt;const N * N: usize&gt;, sorry, not supported. Multiplication and more complex operations don&rsquo;t work on the type level yet.&rdquo;
Also, you can&rsquo;t do heap allocation in const context. You can&rsquo;t write const fn create_buffer() -&gt; Vec&lt;u8&gt;, because Vec is a runtime thing.
&ldquo;What do you do when you hit these issues?&ldquo;&ldquo;Work around it,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Pre-compute constants. If you need N*N, compute it outside first: const SIZE: usize = 4; const SQUARED: usize = SIZE * SIZE; then use Matrix&lt;SIZE, SQUARED&gt;. The compiler isn&rsquo;t dumb — it knows what SQUARED is.&rdquo;
He also reminded me that const generics increases compile time because each different constant value generates a new code instance. But this trade-off is usually worth it, since runtime is faster.
What Did We Get Out of All This? The coffee got cold, but we were still chatting hot. &ldquo;To summarize,&rdquo; I helped him organize his thoughts, &ldquo;what does const generics give us?&rdquo;
He counted on his fingers:
&ldquo;First, massive code reduction. No need to write the same code twice — the DRY principle finally stands up in array processing.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Second, massive performance gains. Stack instead of heap, compile-time optimization instead of runtime computation, SIMD vectorization instead of serial loops. Everything that can be saved, is saved.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Third, type safety. The compiler checks array sizes for us, catches bugs at compile time, no waiting for production to crash.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Fourth, maintainability. Before, fixing bugs in 16 places. Now, fix one place. New hires can read the code, no more getting lost in macro expansions.&rdquo;
He paused, then added: &ldquo;What got me most emotional was our new grad. He used to get scalp migraines looking at macro-generated code. Now seeing const generics syntax, he said, &lsquo;This is just regular generics, I can read this.&rsquo; I almost cried hearing that.&rdquo;
So When Should You Use It? &ldquo;So when should I use this feature?&rdquo; I asked finally.
He thought about it: &ldquo;When you meet these conditions: array size is fixed and known at compile time; you care about performance and want zero-copy zero-allocation; your code starts having lots of repeated patterns, and not writing macros hurts, but writing macros hurts more.&rdquo;
&ldquo;On the flip side, if your array size is user input, or you&rsquo;re still in rapid prototyping phase, don&rsquo;t use it yet. const generics makes compilation slower, and it doesn&rsquo;t help with sizes determined at runtime.&rdquo;
&ldquo;At the end of the day,&rdquo; he summarized, &ldquo;it&rsquo;s a tool, not a silver bullet. Used in the right place, it&rsquo;s a godsend. Used in the wrong place, it&rsquo;s a burden.&rdquo;
I looked out the window — it was dark. &ldquo;Before we go,&rdquo; I asked, &ldquo;how&rsquo;s that cryptography library doing now?&rdquo;
He stood up and stretched. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s doing great. 67% fewer bugs, new hires can get up to speed in three months. Our team can finally have lunch together instead of working overtime fixing bugs every day.&rdquo;
He patted my shoulder: &ldquo;Listen, if you have a lot of fn process_16, fn process_32 functions in your code, try switching to const generics. You&rsquo;ll come back and thank me.&rdquo;
Cheat Sheet Remember This One Line: const generics lets type parameters carry &ldquo;size&rdquo; — like tailor-made clothing, precise to the millimeter.
Use It When:
The array size is fixed and known at compile time You&rsquo;re a performance freak and want zero-copy zero-allocation You catch yourself using macros to copy-paste Don&rsquo;t Use It When:
Size is determined at runtime by user input Your project already compiles painfully slow You&rsquo;re in rapid prototyping mode Lessons Learned the Hard Way:
Can&rsquo;t do complex arithmetic in const parameters — the type system isn&rsquo;t that smart yet const functions can&rsquo;t do heap allocation — they&rsquo;re compile-time only Each different constant value generates new code (trading compile time for execution time) Found This Article Helpful?
Like: If it helped, give it a like so more people can see Share: Share with friends or colleagues who might need it Follow: Follow Monster Programming for more practical tech articles Comment: Have you ever had to write N similar functions in your project? How did you solve it? Your support is what keeps me going!

Xiao Li Rewrote Go Monolith to Rust Axum, Company Saved $800K+ Yearly, His Salary Hit $2M That Unexpected Salary Increase Last week Xiao Li asked me out for dinner. The guy&rsquo;s usually tight with money, so if he&rsquo;s treating, something&rsquo;s up.
After a few drinks, he finally spilled: &ldquo;I rewrote our backend from Go to Rust. Saved the company over 800K a year, and they bumped my salary to 2 million.&rdquo;
I nearly dropped my chopsticks. I know Xiao Li&rsquo;s a solid developer, but this seemed way too good to be true.
&ldquo;Don&rsquo;t get ahead of yourself,&rdquo; Xiao Li said, downing his beer. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s not that simple. Most rewrite projects end up being career trapdoors—slow progress, high risk, endless blame games. I got out unscathed because the conditions happened to align perfectly.&rdquo;
How Broken Was Their Original System When Xiao Li took over the Go monolith, it had been running for years. The code wasn&rsquo;t terrible, not a complete mess. But it was—how do I put it—tired.
&ldquo;Like that fridge you&rsquo;ve had for five or six years,&rdquo; Xiao Li said. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s not broken, but it hums louder than before, uses more electricity, and doesn&rsquo;t cool as well. You say it&rsquo;s broken? Still works. You say it&rsquo;s good? Never feels right.&rdquo;
Check out the numbers:
Peak capacity: 28,000 requests per second About 3 failures per week Monthly infrastructure costs: 130K+ An unspoken &ldquo;don&rsquo;t touch that code&rdquo; culture forming in the team Most failures weren&rsquo;t catastrophic outages. They were latency spikes, partial service slowdowns, retry storms. Users noticed slowness but didn&rsquo;t bother calling support. These are the worst kind of problems—not serious enough to get budget approval for fixes, but draining team energy constantly.
[Client] | [Load Balancer] | [Go Monolith] | | | Auth Search Billing | [Main Database] Everything was挤在一起. Optimize search, gotta worry about affecting billing. Ship a new feature, test the whole system. Scale up, and every module scales together—even though only search needed more resources.
&ldquo;This architecture isn&rsquo;t unusable,&rdquo; Xiao Li said. &ldquo;But once you hit a certain scale, money starts pouring out. Through microservices architecture transformation, we can achieve finer-grained resource management and more efficient performance optimization.&rdquo;
That&rsquo;s why Xiao Li decided on the Go-to-Rust migration.
Those Three Painful Months Learning Rust Xiao Li didn&rsquo;t jump into production code right away. The first three months were all self-study at nights and weekends—nothin change to the company&rsquo;s codebase.
Coming from Go to Rust, some things felt especially awkward. Go&rsquo;s concurrency model—spin up a goroutine and you&rsquo;re done, runtime handles scheduling. Not Rust—you gotta explicitly tell it when this task runs, where it runs, and who owns the data.
&ldquo;At first I thought Rust was just making my life difficult,&rdquo; Xiao Li said. &ldquo;After a while, I realized it&rsquo;s actually pulling out the complexity Go hides in the runtime. Go&rsquo;s scheduler is excellent, but you can&rsquo;t see or modify the decisions it makes for you. Rust makes you face those decisions head-on—exhausting, sure, but when things go wrong, you know exactly where to look.&rdquo;
This Go-to-Rust rewrite process gave Xiao Li a deep appreciation for the differences between the two languages.
A concrete example: In Go, when a goroutine blocks, the runtime automatically switches. You might have no idea where the blocking happened until traffic spikes one day and everything freezes. Rust&rsquo;s Tokio won&rsquo;t hide these issues for you—a blocking call will just deadlock the worker thread, and you&rsquo;ll spot it immediately.
&ldquo;That &lsquo;won&rsquo;t hide your problems from you&rsquo; design philosophy,&rdquo; Xiao Li said, &ldquo;saved us several times later. It&rsquo;s the key reason this performance optimization succeeded.&rdquo;
The First Cut: Search Service Only Xiao Li&rsquo;s team didn&rsquo;t go for a big-bang rewrite. First cut was just the search service.
Why search? Several reasons: read-heavy, simple state; clean API boundaries, no other modules affected; most resource-intensive at the time; quick rollback to Go version if things went wrong.
[Client] | [Load Balancer] | [Go Monolith] ---&gt; [Rust Axum Search Service] | [Legacy Database] Two systems ran in parallel for a month. Traffic mirroring compared results, ensuring the Rust version returned identical data to the Go version.
The result surprised even Xiao Li: search throughput jumped from 28K to 120K requests per second—about a 4.7x improvement, with more stable P99 latency under high load.
But Xiao Li was honest about it: &ldquo;Those numbers have水分. The new system had more aggressive caching, shorter request paths, and a data model optimized for search. Can&rsquo;t simply say Rust is 4.7x faster than Go.&rdquo;
Regardless, this microservices architecture transformation delivered significant performance optimization gains.
What really thrilled him wasn&rsquo;t the speed—it was the stability. Before, traffic peaks would trigger retry storms, then the whole system would shake. After switching to Rust, the search service under pressure was特别 boring. Just ran steadily, no drama.
&ldquo;Boring,&rdquo; Xiao Li said, &ldquo;is the highest compliment in backend engineering.&rdquo;
A Year of Gradual Refactoring After three months of stable search service operation, leadership started asking: should we migrate the rest?
What Xiao Li&rsquo;s team did for the next year can be summed up in one sentence: always keep both legs running.
Stage 1: [Client] -&gt; [Load Balancer] -&gt; [Go Monolith] Stage 2: [Client] -&gt; [Load Balancer] -&gt; [Go Mononym] -&gt; [Rust Microservices] Stage 3: [Client] -&gt; [Load Balancer] -&gt; [Rust Microservices] -&gt; [Go Residuals] Through this gradual Go-to-Rust migration strategy, they successfully built a microservices architecture, achieving significant cost optimization.
&ldquo;Istio was huge in this process,&rdquo; Xiao Li said. &ldquo;We could shift traffic by percentage, rollback at the mesh layer if something went wrong—no code redeployment needed.&rdquo;
OpenTelemetry was another hero. They had tracing in Go too, but Rust spans have a characteristic—their lifecycle binds to code structure. Resource leaks, blocking calls, request fanouts are all visible at a glance. Problem-solving changed from being detective work to reading a map.
This observability changed how they handled failures. Before: &ldquo;Something&rsquo;s wrong again, what are the symptoms?&rdquo; Now: &ldquo;This spot on the graph is anomalous, check if it&rsquo;s that logic.&rdquo;
The Final Numbers After completing the migration:
Metric Go Monolith Rust Microservices Peak Throughput 28K RPS 187K RPS Monthly Infrastructure 130K 30K Annual Savings - ~800K Production Failures ~3/week 14 months zero Dropping from 130K to 30K means an 800K difference annually. But this isn&rsquo;t just &ldquo;Rust is fast&rdquo;—it&rsquo;s the result of several factors叠加: predictable async scheduling, no hidden runtime overhead; Axum middleware supports explicit backpressure control; services broken down for better fault isolation; cache hit rate up, retries down, achieving significant performance optimization and cost optimization.
There were costs too. Compilation time got longer. Hiring Rust developers is harder than hiring Go engineers. New team members need more systems programming background. Some internal tools they left as-is, continued using Go, because rewriting them wasn&rsquo;t worth it.
Why This Rewrite Turned Into a Salary Increase &ldquo;The raise isn&rsquo;t because Rust is amazing,&rdquo; Xiao Li said. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s because this rewrite changed a few things that really matter to the company.&rdquo;
The 800K annual cost savings is auditable—finance can see it directly on the bills. The team previously exhausted by three weekly failures now has that human energy释放. New product features don&rsquo;t come with &ldquo;might bring down the backend&rdquo; anxiety. Capacity planning meetings changed from &ldquo;how many machines to add&rdquo; to &ldquo;current resources will last a year.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Most rewrites stop at &lsquo;it runs,&rsquo;&rdquo; Xiao Li said. &ldquo;This rewrite changed the company&rsquo;s cost structure and operational rhythm. That&rsquo;s what leadership notices. The raise isn&rsquo;t a reward—it&rsquo;s the company revaluing my economic contribution.&rdquo;
Things He Wouldn&rsquo;t Do Again Xiao Li said if he could do it over, some things he&rsquo;d handle differently.
First, he underestimated the social costs of rewriting. Team members worried about being sidelined, felt their code was being invalidated. These emotions needed addressing, but he was too focused on the tech side.
Second, Rust isn&rsquo;t suitable for all scenarios. Admin interfaces, low-frequency batch tasks—he wouldn&rsquo;t touch those now. Rewriting them had too little reward for the risk. This rewrite experience taught him that choosing the right scenarios matters more than the technology itself.
Third, no service mesh and distributed tracing, don&rsquo;t start the Go-to-Rust migration. They achieved zero-downtime switches thanks to Istio and OpenTelemetry. Without this infrastructure, &ldquo;smooth migration&rdquo; is just empty words.
Should You Do It &ldquo;I asked Xiao Li before leaving: so should our company try this too?&rdquo; I recalled the dinner scene.
He asked me back: &ldquo;Does it hurt now?&rdquo;
If your system runs stably, costs are acceptable, and the team isn&rsquo;t exhausted, then rewriting is creating problems, not solving them. Not all systems need rewriting—most actually don&rsquo;t.
Go-to-Rust migration like this makes sense only when the business feels the pain and is willing to shoulder a new risk to eliminate the old one, while expecting real returns through this performance optimization and cost optimization.
&ldquo;Our situation happened to fit these conditions,&rdquo; Xiao Li said. &ldquo;What about yours?&rdquo;
Found this article useful?
If your company is considering a tech stack migration, or you&rsquo;re interested in real production comparisons between Rust and Go, feel free to like and share.
Questions or thoughts? Let&rsquo;s discuss in the comments. What kind of technical debt has your team encountered? How did you finally handle it?
